Implementing a text editor with multiple lists using Angular involves several steps, including setting up the basic components, creating custom editors, and managing data bindings. Below is an example of how you might approach this:

### Step 1: Set Up Your Project

First, make sure you have Angular CLI installed. If not, install it:
```bash
npm install -g @angular/cli
```
Then create a new Angular project if you haven't already:
```bash
ng new text-editor-project
cd text-editor-project
```

### Step 2: Create Components for Text Editor and List

Create two components, `TextEditorComponent` and `ListComponent`.

#### `text-editor.component.ts`
```typescript
import { Component } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-text-editor',
  templateUrl: './text-editor.component.html',
  styleUrls: ['./text-editor.component.css']
})
export class TextEditorComponent {
  content = '';
}
```

#### `list-component.component.ts`
```typescript
import { Component } from '@angular/core';
import { TextEditorService } from '../text-editor.service'; // Assuming you have a service to communicate between components

@Component({
  selector: 'app-list',
  templateUrl: './list.component.html'
})
export classListComponent {
  items: string[] = [];

  constructor(private textEditorService: TextEditorService) {}

  addItem(item: string): void {
    this.items.push(item);
  }

  removeItem(index: number): void {
    this.items.splice(index, 1);
  }
}
```

#### `text-editor.component.html`
```html
<input [(ngModel)]="content" placeholder="Enter text here..." />
<button (click)="addToList()">Add to List</button>
<ul>
  <li *ngFor="let item of items">{{item}}</li>
</ul>

<textarea [(ngModel)]="editorContent"></textarea>
```

#### `list.component.html`
```html
<mat-card *ngIf="items.length > 0">
  <div mat-line *ngFor="let item of items">
    {{item}}
    <button (click)="removeItem($index)">Remove</button>
  </div>
</mat-card>
```

### Step 3: Implement Data Binding Between Components

In your `app.module.ts`, import and declare services that will handle communication between the components.

#### `app.module.ts`
```typescript
import { NgModule } from '@angular/core';
import { BrowserModule } from '@angular/platform-browser';
import { AppComponent } from './app.component';
import { TextEditorComponent } from './text-editor/text-editor.component';
import { ListComponent } from './list/list.component';
import { TextEditorService } from './text-editor.service';

@NgModule({
  declarations: [
    AppComponent,
    TextEditorComponent,
    ListComponent
  ],
  imports: [
    BrowserModule
  ],
  providers: [TextEditorService],
  bootstrap: [AppComponent]
})
export class AppModule { }
```

### Step 4: Implement Services for Communication

Create a service to handle data exchange between the components.

#### `text-editor.service.ts`
```typescript
import { Injectable } from '@angular/core';

@Injectable({
  providedIn: 'root'
})
export class TextEditorService {

  private editorContent = '';

  addTextToEditor(content: string) {
    this.editorContent += content;
  }

  getEditorContent(): string {
    return this.editorContent;
  }
}
```

